  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "JtPr0eKWcGSgIEuOuTANnIPZURE=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-20T08:46:06.145Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Flowable and Camunda arrived at the same place technically and diverge entirely on licence. Flowable Enterprise is sold on-premise by a Swiss vendor with a German office under ISO 27001, so a public buyer can simply buy it: dead end. Camunda offers an archived Apache-2.0 build with no security patches after October 2025 and a maintained build whose licence permits only non-production use, with a key that may not be circumvented: the wedge survives, relocated from auditability to procurement.\n\nThe same logic runs through the other verdicts. n8n's technical gap is real, and its licence adds a second, independent exclusion \u2014 not OSI-approved, so tenders requiring open source cannot count it. Odoo's audit is LGPL and default-on, which removes the wedge rather than creating one.\n\nWhere a capability exists in every product in a category, the discriminator moves to the terms on which it can be acquired. That is a different research question from the one the seven-question screen was built to answer, and it should be asked explicitly rather than discovered late.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-20T08:46:06.145642Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-20T08:46:06.145646Z"
    }
  },
